




:: warrior_queen_day_5_1
<<set $warrior_queen_arc_step = 5>>
Steel meets steel in the grey half-light, and for three glorious seconds, I am winning. The rapier's point drives toward Aldric's chest, a clean thrust from the terza guard, rising, powered by the hips and the core and the deepening instinct that is beginning, just barely, to replace the conscious effort of five days ago. The blade moves as though it has opinions of its own, as though the steel and the hand and the body and the breath are all in agreement for once, a rare parliament of aligned intent.

Aldric parries. The practice blade deflects the thrust with the contemptuous economy of a man swatting a wasp, but the parry is //late//. A fraction of a second, perhaps a quarter-beat of a hummingbird's wing, but late. The rapier has been //faster// than his response.

I feel the moment register in his grey eyes before his expression seals it away. Surprise. Not shock, Aldric is beyond shock, but the subtle recalibration of a man adjusting his expectations upward.

<<speech "aldric">>The thrust is improving. The hip rotation is cleaner today. What changed?<</speech>>

I know what changed but cannot speak it aloud. The breast guard. Worn beneath the training corset, worn beneath the leathers, the boiled leather cups flattening and shielding the breast tissue with a compression tighter and more specific than the corset alone provided. The breasts, immobilised beneath two layers of support, breast guard and corset together, have become, for the first time since they appeared, genuinely //invisible// during movement. No bounce. No shift. No lateral migration during the pivot. No upward surge during the lunge. The tissue exists, aches, grows, but mechanically it is //contained// with a completeness that frees the body to move without the perpetual, low-grade anxiety of //what are they doing now//.

<<speech "mc">>Better support.<</speech>>

<<speech "aldric">>Veletia's doing?<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>Yseldra's. The breast guard she commissioned. Combined with the training corset, it...<</speech>>

I search for a word that is not //eliminated// and is not //hid// and is not //denied//. A word that acknowledges the flesh without pretending it does not exist.

<<speech "mc">>...integrated. The chest is part of the system now. Not fighting it.<</speech>>

Aldric gives the millimetre-nod, his version of a standing ovation.

<<speech "aldric">>Good. The body should not fight itself. Each part has a purpose. Each part...<</speech>>

He stops. His grey eyes shift past my shoulder toward the yard's entrance, and something in his expression changes. Not softened, Aldric's face is structurally incapable of softening, but //adjusted//. A subtle rearrangement of the granite, as though the mountain has noticed an approaching force and decided to take it seriously. I turn.

[[Veletia arrives|warrior_queen_day_5_2]]

:: warrior_queen_day_5_2

Mistress Veletia Roswyn enters the Iron Garden the way a sunrise enters a valley, gradually, inevitably, and with an intensity of colour that makes everything else look grey by comparison. She is resplendent. The word exists for women like Veletia, women who wear silk the colour of crushed elderberries at dawn in a training yard and make it look like a deliberate aesthetic choice rather than a catastrophic misjudgment of venue. Her fiery red curls are piled high and pinned with what appear to be two golden hairpins shaped like miniature swords, a concession, presumably, to the martial setting. She carries a garment over one arm with the reverence a priestess might reserve for a sacred relic.

<<speech "mc">>Veletia. It is before dawn.<</speech>>

<<speech "veletia">>Beauty does not wait for the sun, darling, and neither does necessity.<</speech>>

She sweeps her gaze across the Iron Garden, the cobblestones, the weapons racks, the training dummies, the sweat-stained evidence of combat, with the expression of a woman who walked into the wrong room at a party and is determined to make it work regardless.

<<speech "veletia">>Sir Aldric.<</speech>>

<<speech "aldric">>Mistress Veletia.<</speech>>

The exchange is brief, formal, and loaded with something I had not expected, mutual respect. Aldric's eyes flicker to Veletia for the barest instant, and in that flicker I read acknowledgment. He respects her. Not as a warrior, she could not hold a blade to save her life, and I suspect the sight of genuine blood would send her reaching for her smelling salts, but as a //specialist//. She understands the female body the way he understands combat, and in this matter, her expertise supersedes his.

<<speech "veletia">>I have brought something.<</speech>>

She unfurls the garment from her arm with a theatrical flourish that would have been excessive at a coronation and is wildly, magnificently inappropriate in a training yard at five in the morning. It is a corset.

Not the training corset I currently wear, the grey, functional, steel-boned garment that Veletia produced three days ago. This is something //else//. Something that exists in the gap between the corset I have and the armour I need.

<<speech "mc">>What is that?<</speech>>

<<speech "veletia">>Your salvation, darling.<</speech>>

[[The combat corset|warrior_queen_day_5_3]]

:: warrior_queen_day_5_3

She holds it up in the pre-dawn light, and despite every instinct of masculine resistance still lingering in the crumbling architecture of the prince, I look.

The exterior is reinforced linen, dyed a practical dark grey that will not show dirt or blood, the same colour as the training corset, but the construction is different. Heavier. More structured. Thin steel boning runs vertically through the torso in eight channels, providing rigid support without imprisonment. The boning is not merely cosmetic or postural, each channel is positioned over a specific muscle group, the obliques, the transverse abdominis, the erector spinae, the serratus anterior. The corset is not merely holding the body together. It is //engaging// it, using the boning to activate the core musculature through gentle, persistent compression.

The chest is the revelation. The cups, and they are truly //cups// now, not the padded panels of the training corset but sculpted, shaped hollows designed to receive and contain, are lined with a material I do not recognise. Not lambskin. Not linen. Something softer, denser, with a faint sheen that catches the light.

<<speech "veletia">>Spider-silk padding. Spun by the weavers in the East Wing from the thread of cave spiders, the large, harmless ones that Edran cultivates in the sub-gardens. Stronger than leather. Softer than cotton, and it breathes, darling, it wicks the sweat away from the breast skin and disperses it through the outer fabric, so the tissue remains dry and the chafing that has been plaguing you beneath your current corset ceases entirely.<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>How do you know about the chafing?<</speech>>

<<speech "veletia">>Because I have eyes, Your Highness, and because you have been adjusting the underarm lacing with increasing frequency each afternoon, which is a tell as clear as a banner on the rampart. The current corset is functional but crude, it was built in haste. This one was built with //intention//.<</speech>>

She turns it in the light. The waist is cinched, not severely, not the punishing hourglass of court fashion, but enough to engage the core musculature. The boning at the waist will compress the obliques and the transverse abdominis, activating them through every movement, every breath, every stance-hold and lunge and pivot. Not just //supporting// the core. //Training// it. Turning the corset into an instrument that builds strength through the act of wearing it.

<<speech "veletia">>The cups are adjustable. See here...<</speech>>

she indicates a nearly invisible seam along the cup's upper edge

<<speech "veletia">>...the panel can be released and replaced with a larger one as the breast tissue grows. I have prepared three sizes. The current cup is for your present development. The second is for where Thalara projects you will be in six weeks. The third...<</speech>>

She pauses. A smile plays at her lips, warm, knowing, and entirely without cruelty.

<<speech "veletia">>The third is for the queen.<</speech>>

Aldric clears his throat. The sound is the tectonic equivalent of a mountain coughing.

<<speech "aldric">>Put it on. I want to see you move in it.<</speech>>

[[The fitting|warrior_queen_day_5_4]]

:: warrior_queen_day_5_4

Veletia laces me into the combat corset in the corner of the Iron Garden, behind the weapons rack, with Aldric standing a respectful fifteen paces away, his arms folded, his gaze fixed on the middle distance with the determined intensity of a man who would rather be anywhere else during this particular procedure.

I strip the old training corset first. The leather chest piece comes off, then the lacing, then the damp grey linen falling away from skin flushed and warm from the morning's drill. Beneath, the breast guard, Yseldra's contribution, sits against the bare flesh, the boiled leather cups dark with sweat.

<<speech "veletia">>Remove the breast guard as well. The combat corset will incorporate its function, the cups are deep enough and rigid enough to provide string deflection. You will not need both layers.<</speech>>

I unbuckle the breast guard. It comes away from the skin with a soft //peel//, the lamb's wool lining releasing the flesh beneath. The breasts, free of both corset and guard, settle against my ribcage with a softness that still startles me. The morning air finds them immediately. The nipples pucker, harden, the areolae tightening into dark, crinkled circles that contract against the cold. Goosebumps chase each other across the mounds in concentric waves, the skin responding to temperature with an involuntary, visible, unmistakably //feminine// cascade of reaction.

I stand half-naked in the Iron Garden. My chest bare, the breasts exposed to the grey dawn and the cold air and the cobblestones and the distant rampart where soldiers pace. The tissue is small, still, still small, barely filling a cupped hand, but //present//. Rounded. Defined. The inframammary fold Veletia measured is deeper now than it was three days ago, the crease marking the point where breast separates from ribcage, the beginning of //hang// rather than //sit//.

<<speech "veletia">>Lift your arms.<</speech>>

I lift my arms. Veletia wraps the combat corset around my torso from behind. The linen is cool against my warm skin, a shock that makes the tissue tighten further, the nipples hardening to their maximum erection, two stiff peaks that meet the spider-silk lining of the cups as Veletia settles the chest panel into position.

The cups //receive// the breasts. The spider-silk is extraordinary. The material cradles the tissue with a softness that is paradoxically supportive, it gives where the breast pushes, but holds where the breast pulls, distributing the compression evenly across the entire surface rather than concentrating it at pressure points the way the old corset had. The nipples, cushioned by the silk's depth, soften from their cold-hardened state into a more relaxed erection, still present, still prominent, but no longer //grinding// against an unyielding surface.

<<speech "veletia">>Now the lacing.<</speech>>

Her hands move quickly. The combat corset's lacing runs up the back in a double-cross pattern, leather ties threaded through reinforced eyelets, each crossing pulling the linen snug against the torso. Veletia tightens in stages, lower back first, then waist, then ribcage, then the band beneath the bust, each stage drawing the corset incrementally tighter, each tightening engaging a new layer of muscle, a new section of the core.

The waist. The cinching compresses the obliques and the transverse abdominis, the deep core muscles Aldric has been trying to awaken through the feminine guard. The boning presses against these muscles through the linen, not painfully but //insistently//, as though politely informing them that their participation is expected at all times, not merely during active stance-holds.

The ribcage. The boning aligns the ribs, correcting the slight lateral shift Thalara's potions are producing as the bone structure reorganises. The compression stabilises the ribcage into the position it will occupy once the transformation is complete, narrower above, wider below, the geometry of a female thorax.

The bust. The final tightening secures the cups against the breast tissue, the spider-silk lining conforming to the exact topography of the mounds, every curve, every bump, every contour of the growing flesh mapped by the material and held in place with a precision that is simultaneously mechanical and //intimate//. The corset settles. Veletia ties the lacing and steps back.

[[The transformation|warrior_queen_day_5_5]]

:: warrior_queen_day_5_5

Something fundamental //changes//. I breathe, and the breath comes from the belly, automatically, because the corset's construction makes chest-breathing structurally inefficient. The boning along the ribcage prevents the full expansion that chest-breathing requires, funnelling the breath downward into the abdomen, into the core, into the deep place where Aldric has spent two days trying to teach me to breathe.

The corset teaches it in three seconds. I stand, and the posture is //different//. Not the prince's broad-shouldered, weight-forward stance. Not the uncertain, wobbling attempt at the feminine guard that characterised my first days. Something //else//. The boning supports the spine. The waist compression engages the core. The cup compression stabilises the breast weight. The entire torso becomes a unified structure, ribcage, waist, hips, breast all held in alignment, each element supporting the others, the whole greater than the sum of its aching, growing parts.

I take a step. Then another. Then a cross-step, left foot over right, hips turning, and the difference is //staggering//.

The wobble is gone. The perpetual, low-grade instability that has plagued every movement since the transformation began, the swimming centre of gravity, the shifting breast weight, the hips that pull the rotation off-axis, the shoulders that no longer drive the stroke, //stabilises//. The corset holds the torso rigid through the transition. The cups keep the breasts from shifting during the cross-step. The boning keeps the core engaged through the pivot. The waist cinching keeps the obliques firing through the rotation.

I feel //rooted//. Not rigid, the corset moves with me, the linen flexing, the boning bending slightly at the engineered flex-points, the cups adjusting as the tissue within them responds to the movement, but //anchored//. The body has a centre again. Not the prince's centre, that was high, in the chest and shoulders. This centre is //low//. In the hips. In the pelvis. In the deep architecture of bone and muscle the transformation is building and the corset is now supporting.

<<speech "mc">>Oh.<</speech>>

The word comes out soft. Breathless. Barely a word at all, more an exhalation of surprise. Veletia's smile is insufferable in its vindication.

<<speech "veletia">>Oh, she says. As though the women of the world have not known this for ten thousand years. As though every woman who has ever run, or fought, or ridden a horse, or done anything more strenuous than sitting upon a cushion, has not wrapped her chest since the dawn of binding cloth.<</speech>>

I am too astonished to argue. Aldric has approached. He stands ten paces away, studying me with the evaluative intensity he usually reserves for weapons and fortifications. His grey eyes track from my feet to my hips to my waist to the corset-supported chest, reading the posture, reading the alignment, reading the //potential//.

<<speech "aldric">>Assume the feminine guard.<</speech>>

I assume the feminine guard. Feet together. Weight in the hips. Core engaged. Spine straight. Breath in the belly.

The stance is //immediate//. Not a struggle, not a negotiation with rebellious muscles and uncertain balance. The corset's compression pre-loads the core engagement. The bust support eliminates the forward-pulling chest weight. The hip width, wider now, wider each day, provides the base, and the corset's waist boning connects the base to the upper body in a continuous chain of supported structure.

I hold the stance. No trembling. No wobble. The legs are strong beneath me, the hips stable, the core a pillar of quiet tension running from pelvis to ribcage without interruption.

Aldric's eyebrows rise by one millimetre. From him, that is the equivalent of falling to his knees and weeping with astonishment.

[[The footwork drills begin|warrior_queen_day_5_6]]

:: warrior_queen_day_5_6

<<speech "aldric">>Today we learn the feet. The stance is the foundation. The footwork is the house built upon it. A foundation without a house is a slab of stone. Useful only to the lichen.<</speech>>

He assumes a position across from me. Practice blade drawn. Eyes steady.

<<speech "aldric">>Three movements. The cross-step, the pivot-retreat, and the drop-step. Learn them and the rapier becomes a hunting hawk, able to strike from any angle, any distance, any position. Fail to learn them and the rapier becomes a garden stake, planted, immobile, and waiting to be yanked from the ground.<</speech>>

He demonstrates the first. The //cross-step//. Leading foot crosses in front of the trailing foot. Hips turn. Body presents a narrow profile. Ground is covered laterally, not a shuffle, not a sidestep, but a //crossing// that winds the hips like a coiled spring and releases them in the opposite direction.

<<speech "aldric">>The cross-step uses the hip width for stability. The wider the hips, the broader the base from which the pivot occurs. Your old lateral movement was a shuffle, flat-footed, plodding, designed for a body with its weight in the thighs. The cross-step moves the weight //through// the hips. Your hips...<</speech>>

He pauses. Lets the observation complete itself without his assistance.

<<speech "aldric">>...are becoming an asset. Use them.<</speech>>

I cross-step. Left foot over right. The hips turn, and the //turn// is different than any rotation I performed as the prince. The wider pelvis generates more rotational momentum. The femurs, angled slightly inward by the pelvis's reshaping, cross more naturally. The trailing foot clears the leading foot without catching, without stumbling, without the tangled awkwardness that characterised my first attempts at lateral movement since the hips began to widen.

The cross-step //works//. Not perfectly. The timing is rough, the rhythm unpolished, the trailing foot slightly late. But the fundamental mechanics, the hip-driven pivot, the narrow profile, the ground-covering lateral motion, function. The body knows what to do. The hips //know//. They have been building toward this movement for weeks, widening and reshaping and tilting, and now, asked to generate the rotation the cross-step demands, they respond with an enthusiasm that is almost //eager//.

<<speech "aldric">>Again. Faster, and keep the blade pointed at me throughout the transition, the cross-step is an offensive reposition, not a retreat. The enemy should see steel at every moment.<</speech>>

I cross-step. Again. Again. The rhythm emerges, step, turn, settle, blade. Step, turn, settle, blade. The corset holds the torso stable through each transition, the boning and the cups maintaining alignment while the lower body spirals beneath.

The second movement. The //pivot-retreat//. Weight drops into the back hip. Front foot lifts and sweeps backward in a crescent. Body spirals away from the attacker's line while the rapier stays extended, a withdrawal that is simultaneously a threat.

I have watched Aldric demonstrate this before. But today, in the combat corset, with the hips that are wider and the centre that is lower and the core that is actively, continuously engaged by the boning's compression...

I pivot. The back hip //catches// the weight. The front foot sweeps. The body spirals, and the rotation is //fast//. Not merely competent, not merely functional, but //fast//. The lower centre of gravity generates rotational momentum that a top-heavy male body could not match. I spin out of the attack line and find myself facing Aldric with the rapier still pointed at his chest, balanced, ready, poised to spring forward.

<<speech "aldric">>Good. The retreat is clean. The blade stayed oriented. Now, the third.<</speech>>

[[The drop-step|warrior_queen_day_5_7]]

:: warrior_queen_day_5_7

The //drop-step//. A sharp, explosive descent. Both knees bending deep. Centre of gravity plunging downward, beneath a high strike, beneath a lateral sweep, beneath whatever killing arc the enemy's blade describes above the collapsing body. Then, eruption. Rising from the crouch into a thrust aimed at belly or groin, the body uncoiling from the earth like a spring released.

This is the movement that will change everything. I do not know this yet. The body does.

<<speech "aldric">>The drop-step is the crown jewel of the feminine combat art. A tall, broad-shouldered man attempting it is slow, awkward, vulnerable in the crouch. His high centre of gravity makes the descent ponderous and the recovery sluggish. But a body with a lower centre, with wider hips for base stability, narrower shoulders for speed, a compact torso supported by the corset and the core...<</speech>>

He pauses. Looks at me.

<<speech "aldric">>...folds into the earth like water and springs from it like a blade. Show me.<</speech>>

I drop. The knees bend. The hips descend. The corset's boning guides the descent, keeping the spine straight, keeping the core engaged, keeping the breast tissue compressed and stable within the cups as the body compresses downward. The movement is fast. Faster than I expect. The lower centre of gravity means less distance to travel, the body does not have to //fall// as far to reach the crouch, and the wider hips provide a stable base at the bottom of the drop that keeps me from toppling.

I am //down//. Crouched, compact, the rapier extended forward. The cobblestones are cold beneath my knees. My thighs burn, the deep muscles the feminine guard is building, the muscles of the lower body the transformation is layering with a woman's distribution of strength, but they hold.

<<speech "aldric">>Now rise.<</speech>>

I rise. Upward. Explosive. The thighs and the hips drive the ascent, not the shoulders, not the arms, but the //base//, the wide, stable, increasingly powerful foundation of the lower body. The rapier extends as I rise, the blade reaching forward, the thrust driven from the hips through the arm through the steel...

The point stops one inch from Aldric's breastplate. The Iron Garden is silent. The mist is burning off. The cobblestones are drying. A crow calls from the rampart.

My rapier's point rests against his heart. His practice blade hangs in the air above the space where my head had been.

He looks down at the blade. He looks at me, crouched half-risen from the drop-step, my hair wild, my cheeks flushed, breath coming hard, the combat corset dark with sweat but holding, //holding//, the rapier an extension of a body that has finally, for one blinding, perfect moment, moved the way it was meant to. The silence lasts three heartbeats.

<<speech "aldric">>Good.<</speech>>

Then, because he is Aldric and praise is a resource more finite than dragonstone in winter,

<<speech "aldric">>Again.<</speech>>

[[Drilling the footwork|warrior_queen_day_5_8]]

:: warrior_queen_day_5_8

We drill until the sun is properly up and the Iron Garden is bathed in golden light that turns the cobblestones from grey to amber.

I stumble. I trip. I cross my own feet and sprawl on the cobblestones with a force that drives the breath from my lungs and sends a sharp ache through both compressed breasts, even the combat corset has limits when the body within it strikes solid stone at speed. The tissue protests, a bright, hot flare of compression-pain as the cups flatten against the ribcage, the breast flesh compressing beyond the comfortable range, the nipples grinding against the spider-silk padding.

I rise. Always rise. The corset holds. The rapier answers my hand. The feminine guard anchors me. Aldric layers combinations. Cross-step into lunge. Pivot-retreat into riposte. Drop-step into rising thrust. Each combination demands that the footwork and the bladework operate simultaneously, the feet moving the body while the hand moves the steel, the two systems synchronised by the core the corset is continuously, relentlessly training.

By midday I am executing the cross-step smoothly enough that Aldric adds resistance, slashing at me with the practice blade, forcing me to cross-step //under duress//. The first ten attempts are disasters. I step too early, too late, too far, not far enough, and take bruises across the shoulder and two across the ribs for my failures.

The eleventh attempt is clumsy but functional. I clear the slash and my rapier's point wavers toward Aldric's chest before drifting wide.

The twentieth is clean. Cross-step, turn, thrust, point at his ribs. On the twenty-fifth attempt, the universe shifts on its axis. Aldric swings high. A vicious overhead slash, the killing stroke, Frostfang's signature, delivered with the practice blade but at full speed. The arc is aimed at my skull, and every instinct the prince ever possessed screams //block//.

I drop. The drop-step engages every muscle the feminine guard has been building. Core firing. Hips bending. Knees descending. The corset holds my torso rigid as I fold beneath the arc of the blade. I hear it whistle through the air above my head, parting my hair, close enough to feel the wind but not the steel, the displaced air ruffling the loose strands that have escaped my braid and lifting them like streamers in a gale.

I rise. Exploding upward from the crouch. The rapier extending like a tongue of silver flame. The thrust driven from the hips through the arm through the blade, the corset's boning channelling the core's power into a single, focused line of intent that terminates with the rapier's point pressed against the precise centre of Aldric's breastplate.

We freeze. The training yard is silent save for the cry of a hawk somewhere above the castle and the distant clatter of a kitchen servant dropping something metallic.

My rapier's point rests against his heart. His practice blade hangs in empty air above the space where my skull had been an instant before.

He looks at the blade. He looks at me. I am crouched half-risen from the drop-step. My hair is wild, the braid entirely defeated, long dark waves falling around a face flushed and sweating and shaped by cheekbones higher than they were a week ago. The combat corset is drenched, the grey linen turned charcoal with sweat, the spider-silk cups dark against the chest. Beneath the corset, the breasts heave with each laboured breath, not visibly, not bouncing, but pressing against the cups with the rhythmic insistence of flesh that is alive and growing and working and //there//. And for the second time in my life, the second time in five days, Sir Aldric Vornhelm nods. The nod.

<<speech "aldric">>Good. Again.<</speech>>

[[Water break   Veletia returns|warrior_queen_day_5_9]]

:: warrior_queen_day_5_9

Veletia has not left the Iron Garden. She retreated to the shaded archway at the yard's entrance, where she watched the entire session with the focused intensity of a woman observing a hypothesis confirmed. When Aldric calls the midday break, she approaches with a water jug and a strip of clean linen.

<<speech "veletia">>You dropped beneath a killing stroke and put a blade to his heart. In a corset I built. In //my// corset.<</speech>>

Her eyes are bright. Not with theatrical excitement, not the performative enthusiasm she usually employs, but with something //real//. Something that lives beneath the silk and the curls and the golden hairpins and reaches the surface only when her work proves itself true.

<<speech "mc">>It was the footwork and the training and...<</speech>>

<<speech "veletia">>It was the corset. Not //merely// the corset, I am not so vain as to claim the garment swings the sword. But the corset provided the infrastructure that made the footwork possible. The core engagement. The breast stabilisation. The postural alignment. Without those, the drop-step is a controlled fall. With them...<</speech>>

She reaches forward and presses her palm flat against the corset's front panel. Against the boning. Against the spider-silk. Against the breast tissue beneath, which I can feel compressing slightly under her hand, the warmth of her palm radiating through the layers.

<<speech "veletia">>...with them, the body is a //machine//. Every component accounted for. Every variable managed. Every ounce of flesh put to work.<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>Including these.<</speech>>

I look down at her hand on my chest. At the cups. At the visible shape of the breasts beneath the sweat-darkened linen, a gentle curve, compressed but undeniable, the contour of flesh becoming increasingly difficult to disguise even beneath layers of armour and corsetry.

<<speech "veletia">>//Especially// these, darling. The breast weight is not a problem to be eliminated. It is a variable to be managed. The corset manages it. The guard manages it, and as the tissue grows, as it //will// grow, enthusiastically, your body has made its intentions regarding breast development abundantly clear, the management will adapt. Larger cups. Different compression. Different materials. The corset will grow with you, Caelan. That is its design.<</speech>>

She removes her hand. Produces a small piece of chalk from a pocket that should not have existed in a garment that sheer.

<<speech "veletia">>Now. Hold still. I need to mark the areas where the fit has shifted during exertion. The left cup has migrated upward by two millimetres, the breast tissue is settling lower as the inframammary fold deepens, and the waist boning needs a quarter-inch adjustment at the oblique to account for the narrowing that has occurred since I took measurements three days ago. Your waist is shrinking, Your Highness. Rapidly.<</speech>>

She chalks marks on the corset's exterior while I stand still, dripping sweat, drinking water, and contemplating the surreal experience of being fitted for a garment whilst standing in a training yard that still bears the scuff marks of a fight I just won.

[[Afternoon   combined weapons drill|warrior_queen_day_5_10]]

:: warrior_queen_day_5_10

The afternoon brings Yseldra. She arrives at the Iron Garden without announcement, simply //appears// at the archery position on the yard's east side, bow in hand, as though she had been there all along and the world was merely catching up to her presence. Aldric positions himself opposite.

<<speech "aldric">>Combined weapons drill. Sword and bow. The first half is mine, rapier and footwork. The second half is hers, archery under fatigue. You will transition between the two without rest. A warrior does not get to choose when the battle shifts from blade to bow. The body must answer both masters with the same readiness.<</speech>>

<<speech "yseldra">>Elbow high. Anchor at the cheek. If the string finds the breast, I will not be sympathetic.<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>You have never been sympathetic about anything in your life, Dame Yseldra.<</speech>>

<<speech "yseldra">>Correct. Begin now.<</speech>>

Aldric attacks. Practice blade sweeping laterally. I cross-step, riposte, am parried, pivot-retreat, lunge, am beaten back, drop-step beneath an overhead cut and rise into a thrust that Aldric deflects with his guard.

<<speech "aldric">>Bow. Now.<</speech>>

I sheathe the rapier. Seize the bow from the rack. Nock an arrow. Draw, elbow high, the feminine draw, the anchor at my cheekbone, the string arcing cleanly above the breast, the combat corset holding the tissue stable as the ribcage expands with the draw's tension. I aim. Release. The arrow hits the third ring.

<<speech "yseldra">>Not good enough. Again. Faster.<</speech>>

I nock. Draw. Release. Second ring.

<<speech "yseldra">>Better. Now sword.<</speech>>

I drop the bow. Draw the rapier. Aldric is already moving, a thrust aimed at my chest. I parry, cross-step, riposte. The exchange lasts twenty seconds. My arms are burning. My shoulder aches from the archery draw. The transition from bow-muscles to sword-muscles is jarring, different grips, different ranges of motion, different demands on shoulders that are narrowing and wrists that are loosening and a chest that is growing.

<<speech "aldric">>Bow.<</speech>>

